text
It should be noted, however, that this method does not give a clear-cut answer if the predicted numbers of infrared- and Raman-active fundamentals are similar for various probable structures. Furthermore, a practical difficulty arises in determining the number of fundamentals from the observed spectrum, since the intensities of overtone and combination bands are sometimes comparable to those of fundamentals when they appear as satellite bands of the fundamental. This is particularly true when overtone and combination bands are enhanced anomalously by Fermi resonance (accidental degeneracy). For example, the frequency of the first overtone of the
equation_inline
\nu_{2}
text
vibration of
equation_inline
CO_{2}
text
(667 cm
equation_inline
^{-1}
text
) is very close to that of the
equation_inline
\nu_{1}
text
vibration (1337 cm
equation_inline
^{-1}
text
). Since these two vibrations belong to the same symmetry species (
equation_inline
\Sigma_{g}^{+}
text
), they interact with each other and give rise to two strong Raman lines at 1388 and 1286 cm
equation_inline
^{-1}
text
. Fermi resonances similar to the resonance observed for CO
equation_inline
_{2}
text
may occur for a number of other molecules. It is to be noted also that the number of observed bands depends on the resolving power of the instrument used. Finally, the molecular symmetry in the isolated state is not necessarily the same as that in the crystalline state (Sec. I-23). Therefore this method must be applied with caution to spectra obtained for compounds in the crystalline state.

